+# Qwen Mods
+
+[English](qwen-mods.md) | [简体中文](qwen-mods.zh-CN.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Custom terminal views need to work with commands, session state and events. Users need a general local extension API for packaging that behavior and UI in independently authored modules. Claude Code Mods is the inspiration, not a compatibility target or a cloud hosting service.
+
+## Design
+
+One runtime belongs to each interactive Config. Explicit `/mods load <directory>` reads a `qwen-mod.json` manifest and imports its JavaScript entry. `/mods enable <directory>` also records the absolute directory in a user-owned registry. `--safe-mode` and `--bare` prevent module loading. Only this registry loads automatically. Projects cannot auto-execute modules through repository settings. `/mods unload <id>`, `/mods disable <id>`, and `/mods reload <id>` control lifecycle. Entry reload uses a fresh module URL. Transitive imports require restarting Qwen after edits.
+
+The version 1 API registers commands, React/Ink components in abovePrompt, belowPrompt and footer slots, session and turn listeners, and native Qwen function hooks. It supplies session usage and local context estimates, transient state, atomic persistent JSON updates, notifications, managed timers, cancellable HTTP fetch and subprocess execution, prompt submission and command dispatch. React and Ink come from the host to avoid duplicate React instances. An explicitly experimental Config escape hatch permits advanced integration with Qwen internals.
+
+Modules are trusted Node.js code with the user's filesystem, process, network and environment access. This is not a sandbox. Loading or enabling a directory is explicit authorization to execute it. Host HTTP supplies no credentials automatically. Cloud integration uses the mod author's endpoints and credentials. Existing Qwen tool permission checks still apply to normal submitted turns. Direct Node access is outside those checks.
+
+Commands enter the existing command service and use its collision and disabled-command handling. Native hooks are attached to the owning session, rebound when it changes, and removed individually. Managed hook dispatch and disabled hooks are reported as unsupported. API state survives entry reload, while persistent storage survives restarts. Cleanup aborts host requests and subprocesses and removes views, commands, timers and hooks. A failing mod registration is rolled back. Runtime error reporting and per-view boundaries keep ordinary mod failures from taking down the interface. Arbitrary trusted code can still terminate or block the process.
+
+## Repository split
+
+Qwen Code owns the generic runtime, API, command loading, UI slots and runtime tests. The separate [richardcsuwandi/qwen-mods](https://github.com/richardcsuwandi/qwen-mods) repository owns deadline, usage bar and context bar modules, a hello starter, their behavior tests and author instructions. Personal modules are not shipped in this repository. Runtime tests create temporary synthetic modules to verify the public contract without depending on the companion repository.
+
+## Files and scope
+
+`packages/cli/src/mods` owns API, registry, runtime and UI bridge. The existing slash command processor gains a loader, AppContainer owns lifecycle, and Composer/Footer render generic slots. The companion repository contains standalone modules and author instructions. The implementation targets interactive Ink. OpenTUI, headless and ACP do not load modules. There is no marketplace, remote installation, full-screen renderer replacement, automatic file watcher, dependency installer or Claude API compatibility promise. Authors can use Node packages installed alongside their entry.
+
+## Validation
+
+Confirm `/mods` is absent in the global baseline. Test manifest validation, explicit loading, persistent enable/disable, cleanup, entry reload, collisions, hook ownership, error rollback, storage concurrency and a separately authored module. Build, typecheck and bundle sequentially. Exercise the bundle in an isolated terminal with the examples, restart, toggle views, reload edited code and unload. No external model requests are needed for these checks. Native hook behavior is verified with focused tests, not claimed as a live provider run.
+
+## Acceptance and open questions
+
+A fourth module must add a command and live UI through only its manifest and entry. Disabling and restarting must keep it unloaded. Existing built-in commands must continue to work. Companion modules must remain independent of CLI implementation imports. The API is an initial version for local use. Distribution and upstream adoption remain future decisions.
