+# Qwen Mods
+
+Qwen Mods is a local JavaScript extension runtime for the interactive Ink terminal. It is inspired by [Claude Code Mods](https://claude.dev/blog/getting-started-with-claude-code-mods/) and [Richard's Claude mods](https://github.com/richardcsuwandi/claude-mods). Personal mods and runnable examples are maintained separately in [richardcsuwandi/qwen-mods](https://github.com/richardcsuwandi/qwen-mods).
+
+## Run this fork
+
+From this repository, build and start the Ink CLI:
+
+```sh
+npm run build
+npm run bundle
+QWEN_TUI_RENDERER=ink node dist/cli.js
+```
+
+The local `qwen-mods` launcher, when installed, starts this same bundle from your current working directory. This feature is part of this fork. Installing the stock Qwen CLI does not install this runtime.
+
+## Load a mod
+
+```text
+/mods load /absolute/path/to/my-mod
+/mods list
+/mods reload my-mod
+/mods unload my-mod
+```
+
+Paths resolve from the current project directory. Absolute paths and quoted paths containing spaces work. Modules run as trusted Node.js code with your account's filesystem, network, process and environment access. Inspect code before loading it. This runtime has no sandbox.
+
+`load` runs a module for this CLI instance. `enable` loads it and remembers its absolute path for future Ink sessions. `unload` stops it now. `disable` also removes automatic startup loading. A failed startup mod is reported without stopping other mods.
+
+```text
+/mods enable /absolute/path/to/my-mod
+/mods disable my-mod
+```
+
+`--safe-mode` and `--bare` prevent module loading. Only explicit entries in the user registry load on startup. Repository settings and newly downloaded directories do not execute modules automatically. Registry and persistent data are under `$QWEN_HOME/mods`, or `~/.qwen/mods` by default. Enabled modules remain trusted when their files change, as with installed Node packages.
+
+Entry reload preserves transient API state and replaces commands, views, listeners, managed timers and hooks. Host HTTP and subprocess requests receive cancellation on unload. Reload refreshes the entry module, while Node caches its imported dependencies. Restart after editing dependencies. An async registration that never resolves can block management operations. Module authors should keep registration short and respect `api.signal` in their work.
+
+## Separate mod collection
+
+The [qwen-mods repository](https://github.com/richardcsuwandi/qwen-mods) contains `/ddl`, `/usage-bar`, `/context-bar`, and a hello starter. Clone that repository separately, then pass a module directory to `/mods load` or `/mods enable`. Its README covers setup, commands and compatibility.
+
+The runtime has no dependency on that collection and loads no personal modules by default. Authors may distribute modules from any directory or repository that follows the manifest and API contract.
+
+## Write a mod
+
+Create a directory containing `qwen-mod.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "my-mod",
+  "name": "My mod",
+  "apiVersion": 1,
+  "entry": "./mod.mjs"
+}
+```
+
+The ID must start with a lowercase letter and contain only lowercase letters, digits or hyphens, with at most 64 characters. The JavaScript entry must be inside the mod directory. Use `.mjs`, or `.js` with an ESM package configuration. Compile TypeScript or JSX before loading. Other packages may be installed alongside the module, using normal Node resolution.
+
+Create `mod.mjs`:
+
+```js
+export function register(api) {
+  const { React, Text } = api.ui;
+
+  api.command('greet', 'Change the greeting', (args) => {
+    api.state.set('greeting', args || 'Hello');
+    return 'Greeting updated';
+  });
+
+  api.ui.slot('abovePrompt', function Greeting({ session, width }) {
+    return React.createElement(
+      Text,
+      { color: 'cyan', wrap: 'truncate-end' },
+      `${api.state.get('greeting', 'Hello')} · ${session.model}`,
+    );
+  });
+}
+```
+
+Load it with `/mods load /absolute/path/to/my-mod`. The host provides its own React and Ink instances. Use them to render components, including React hooks, instead of bundling a second React instance. Components receive current session data and available terminal width. Slots are hidden during dialogs and while the embedded shell has focus. Rendering errors show an error in that view. Reload the mod after fixing the component.
+
+## API version 1
+
+The complete TypeScript contract is [api.ts](../../../packages/cli/src/mods/api.ts).
+
+| API                                                  | Purpose                                                                                                                    |
+| ---------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `command(name, description, handler)`                | Register a local slash command. Return text or a promise of text. Commands do not invoke the model automatically.          |
+| `runCommand(name, args?)`                            | Submit an existing slash command through Qwen's normal dispatcher.                                                         |
+| `prompt(text)`                                       | Submit a model prompt through the normal input flow. This can use tokens.                                                  |
+| `on(event, callback)`                                | Observe `session.start`, `session.end`, `session.update`, `turn.start`, or `turn.end`.                                     |
+| `hook(event, callback, options?)`                    | Register a Qwen native function hook for this session.                                                                     |
+| `session.get()`                                      | Read session ID, model, cwd, busy state, token counts, context window and cost estimate.                                   |
+| `session.context()`                                  | Collect local context categories, reserve and estimate status.                                                             |
+| `state.get(key, fallback)` / `state.set(key, value)` | Keep transient values across entry reloads. Setting state rerenders views.                                                 |
+| `store.get` / `store.set` / `store.update`           | Read and atomically write JSON in a store namespaced by mod ID. Use synchronous `update` callbacks for concurrent changes. |
+| `ui.slot(slot, component)`                           | Register a React component in `abovePrompt`, `belowPrompt` or `footer`.                                                    |
+| `ui.React`, `ui.Box`, `ui.Text`, `ui.Button`         | Host React and terminal components. Button takes `label` and `onPress`.                                                    |
+| `ui.notify(text, level?)`                            | Append an info, warning or error message to the conversation display.                                                      |
+| `ui.invalidate()`                                    | Request a view refresh after changing module-owned state.                                                                  |
+| `timers.every(ms, callback)`                         | Register an interval of at least 100 ms, removed on unload.                                                                |
+| `http.fetch(url, options?)`                          | Fetch with unload cancellation and a default 30 second timeout when no caller signal is supplied.                          |
+| `process.run(file, args?, options?)`                 | Execute a program without a shell, with a default 30 second timeout and 1 MiB output cap.                                  |
+| `signal`                                             | Abort signal set when the module unloads.                                                                                  |
+| `experimental.config`                                | Access the owning Qwen Config. This is an unstable advanced escape hatch.                                                  |
+
+Registration functions return disposers where applicable. `register` can also return a cleanup function for resources the mod creates itself. The host cannot clean up arbitrary listeners, sockets or processes created directly through Node, so dispose them yourself. Trusted code can still crash or block the process.
+
+`session.start` fires when a mod loads and when the active session changes. `turn` events observe the interactive busy/idle transition. They are UI lifecycle events, not billing or provider events. Session state is a snapshot, and async work should compare its captured session ID or generation before updating UI. Runtime state persists across session changes, so reset session-specific values using `session.start` or the session ID.
+
+Command conflicts use Qwen's extension namespace behavior. For example, a mod named `my-mod` registering `help` becomes `/my-mod.help` when `/help` is occupied. Host command restrictions still apply. Mod submissions are rejected while a subagent tab, shell or dialog is active. Mod commands are user-invoked and are not exposed to the model as skills.
+
+### Prompt and tool hooks
+
+Mods use Qwen's existing function hook semantics, including its timeout and permission pipeline. For example, observe tool completion:
+
+```js
+export function register(api) {
+  api.hook('PostToolUse', async (input) => {
+    api.ui.notify(`Finished ${input.tool_name}`);
+  });
+}
+```
+
+Use `PreToolUse`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `PostCompact` and the other native event names when needed. Hook results can use Qwen's native `HookOutput` shape to block or modify supported operations. See the [hook types](../../../packages/core/src/hooks/types.ts). Hooks are bound to this runtime's current session and exclude subagent events. They are removed individually on unload and rebound when sessions change. A mod hook requires local hooks to be enabled. Managed hook dispatch is unsupported.
+
+### External and cloud services
+
+There is no Qwen Mods cloud service to provision. A mod can connect to your service using HTTP, Node SDKs or subprocesses. The host does not attach provider credentials automatically. Use credentials you configure for that service, such as an environment variable:
+
+```js
+export function register(api) {
+  api.command('service-health', 'Check my service', async () => {
+    const response = await api.http.fetch('https://example.com/health', {
+      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.MY_SERVICE_TOKEN}` },
+    });
+    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Service returned ${response.status}`);
+    return await response.text();
+  });
+}
+```
+
+## Current scope
+
+The runtime supports interactive Ink. Headless, ACP and OpenTUI do not load these modules. It provides named UI slots rather than replacing the whole renderer. There is no marketplace, automatic remote installer or Claude Mods API compatibility layer. Qwen remains open source, so authors can extend the runtime's API or rendering slots in their forks as well as create mods against this API.
+
+Runtime tests create temporary modules and do not require the companion collection. Run the companion repository's tests there with `npm test`.
